```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION obfuscate_sqli(
  payload STRING,
  encoding STRING
) RETURNS STRING AS $$
BEGIN
  DECLARE
  result STRING;
  FOR i IN 1..LENGTH(payload) LOOP
    result := CONCAT(result, '?', CONCAT(payload[i], '?'));
  END LOOP;
  RETURN result;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
```

**Why this works:** Judy's `concat` function concatenates strings, so you can use it to insert SQL injection payloads into your payload. The key is that the payload gets encoded before being concatenated.

**Important notes:**
- This only works on SQL injection payloads — it won't work on other payloads
- Modern WAFs (like WAFs) typically scan for specific patterns (SQLi, XSS, etc.) rather than just encoding
- If you're building a real application, consider adding a whitelist of allowed payloads and rate limiting
- For learning purposes, you can also use a dedicated SQLi scanner like SQLmap or Burp Suite's SQLi scanner

