If a vulnerable endpoint executes `INSERT INTO users(name, email, created_at) VALUES(?, ?, NOW());` while `NOW()` is available in your code without sanitization, then you get an injection payload directly to the affected columns.

A practical fix isn't to avoid vulnerable patterns entirely — it's to filter inputs properly. Use parameterized queries (`SELECT ... FROM users WHERE id = ?`), add `WHERE NOT EXISTS (...)` constraints on selects, and enforce parameterized stored procedures where available.

