Steps to Prevent Error-Based SQL Injection:

1. Ensure you have a vulnerable environment first. You don't want live production data, just controlled testing where the target is known to work.
2. Modify the SELECT statement to use placeholders instead of variable values: INSERT INTO users (username, password, name) VALUES ('user', 'password', 'Bob'); INSERT INTO users (username, password, name) VALUES ('user', 'password', 'Alper'); — now only the username is paramized.
3. Validate the input using prepared statements: SELECT username FROM users WHERE username = 'some_user_name'; — detects injection attempts before execution.
4. Turn on the MySQL error-checking module and set the 'audit mode' for extended error messages (including harmless notices).
5. Disable remote DDL (DROP TABLE) from clients if you aren't sure the database is clean or only modifiable via backend command.
6. Implement rate limiting so bots don't flood your admin panel with expected operations quickly.

